endobj
128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 58 0 R
/E ( Over recent decades, mental health care has undergone a substantive shift from predominantly clinical, pathology oriented models toward more holistic and integrative approaches that recognise the significance of diversity, lived experience, cultural context, and trauma-informed practice in recovery processes. This evolution has emerged in response to sustained critique of traditional biomedical frameworks, which have been shown to inadequately capture the complexity of individual experience, particularly among historically marginalised & minoritised populations \(Browne et al., 2016; Herman, 1997\). In contrast, integr-     -ative recovery models emphasise collaboration, cultural respect, and empowerment through voluntary participation in care, reframing recovery as a personalised and socially situated process rather than solely a clinical outcome \(Topor et al., 2011\).)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 58 0 R
/E (Contemporary literature increasingly positions voluntary, trauma-informed, and culturally responsive care as central to ethical and effective mental health practice. Within this body of work, lived experience is recognised not only as an outcome of recovery but also as a critical source of knowledge that enhances understanding of recovery dynamics, service engagement, and long-term wellbeing.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 58 0 R
/T (Mental Health Diversity and Recovery Frameworks:)
/E (Mental Health Diversity and Recovery Frameworks:)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 58 0 R
/E ( The concept of mental health diversity foregrounds the existence of multiple, context-dependent pathways to recovery shaped by cultural, social, economic, and personal identities \(Sue et al., 2012\). Historically, mental health services have largely been developed within Western epistemological traditions that prioritise biomedical explan- -ations and standardised treatment protocols \(Kleinman, 1988\). While these approaches have contributed to advances in diagnosis and treatment, scholars argue that they often marginalise individuals from non-Western, Indigenous, and racially minoritised backgrounds whose explanatory models of distress and healing diverge from dominant paradigms \(Gone, 2013\). Empirical research highlights the role of structural inequality, cultural discon-   -nection, & systemic discrimination in exacerbating mental health disparities \(Williams & Mohammed, 2009\). These findings suggest that interventions grounded in cultural meaning systems & community-based values may enhance both engagement and outcomes. As such, recovery-oriented frameworks increasingly call for mental health services that are flexible, pluralistic, and responsive to diverse lived realities. Recovery models informed by lived experience, including the Personal Recovery paradigm, shift the focus from symptom reduction toward meaningful life particip-    -ation, self-determination, and resilience \(Anthony, 1993; Leamy et al., 2011\). These models challenge clinician-led definitions of recovery by privileging the narratives and priorities of individuals with lived experience. Central to this philosophy is the alignment with voluntary care, which emphasises autonomy, shared decision-making, and consumer choice in the development of care plans \(Ridgway, 2001\). Evidence suggests that when individuals are positioned as active partners in their care, they report higher satisfaction, enhanced self-efficacy, and improved long-term outcomes \(Slade et al., 2014\).)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 58 0 R
/E (2. To examine the impact of societal perception and stigma on self-identity and recovery.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
