<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (identity, relationality, and peer support \(Mead & MacNeil, 2006\). The inclusion of peer-led services and service-user involvement in program design has become a defining feature of recovery-oriented mental health systems \(Davidson et al., 2006\). Qualitative studies consistently highlight the importance of trusting therapeutic relation-        -ships, flexible and responsive service models, & recognition of individual and cultural strengths in supporting recovery \(Repper & Carter, 2011\). Case study methodologies within lived-experience research further illuminate the interaction between personal meaning-making processes and formal care structures. Such studies demonstrate that integrative approaches when voluntary, trauma informed, & culturally attuned can enhance self efficacy, support identity recon-     -struction &reduce reliance on crisis-driven interventions \(Topor et al., 2011\). This body of work positions lived experience not merely as a supplementary perspective but as an epistemological foundation for intervention design, service evaluation, and mental health policy development.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (Trauma informed care \(TIC\) has emerged as a response to this concern, advocating for the systematic integration of trauma knowledge into all aspects of service delivery \(Fallot & Harris, 2009\). Core principles of TIC include safety, trustworthiness, choice, collaboration, and empowerment \(SAMHSA, 2014\), which closely align with voluntary and recovery oriented models of care. A growing body of evidence indicates that trauma-informed approaches are associated with improved engagement, reduced use of coercive practices, and decreased risk of retraumatisation across diverse settings, including inpatient psychiatric services, community mental health programs, and subs-       -tance use recovery contexts \(Isobel & Edwards, 2015\). Importantly, TIC is increasingly conceptualised not merely as an individual clinician competency but as an organisa-     -tional framework requiring systemic change to policies, environments, and power structures \(Menschner & Maul, 2016\). As such, trauma-informed practice is now widely regarded as foundational to ethical, person centred mental health care.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 59 0 R
/T (Culturally Responsive Care: Beyond Cultural Com-  -petence:)
/E (Culturally Responsive Care: Beyond Cultural Com-  -petence:)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
