endobj
148 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E ( Collectively, the literature converges on the conclusion that effective mental health care must integrate diversity, trauma awareness, and cultural responsiveness within voluntary, recovery-oriented frameworks that centre lived experience. Despite this consensus, significant gaps remain. Many mental health systems continue to be structured around clinician driven pathology focused models that insufficiently account for personhood, context, and agency. Additionally, the integration of Indigenous and non-Western healing traditions within mainstream services remains limited, often constrained by institutional barriers and epistemic inequities \(Kirmayer et al., 2011\). Furthermore, while integrative recovery models are increasingly advocated, robust longitudinal and comparative evaluation studies remain relatively scarce. There is a pressing need for methodologically rigorous research that examines long-term outcomes of voluntary, trauma-informed, and culturally responsive approaches across diverse populations & service contexts. In summary, contemporary mental health schola-  -rship reflects a paradigmatic shift toward holistic, person-centred care that is voluntary, trauma-informed, and culturally responsive. Such approaches are essential for honouring mental health diversity and supporting authentic recovery pathways as articulated by individuals with lived experience. An integrative framework does not reject clinical expertise but situates it within broader ethical, relational, and cultural contexts that prioritise dignity, agency, and meaning. Ongoing research, policy innovation, and service redesign are required to realise mental health systems that are equitable, inclusive, and genuinely recovery oriented.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
