/Title (Mental health diversity encompasses a wide range of psychological, neurological, physical, social, and cultural experiences that shape how individuals understand them-    -selves, interact with others, & navigate daily life. Although contemporary mental health discourse increasingly ackno-  -wledges trauma-informed and person-centred approaches, dominant biomedical models often remain limited in their capacity to account for the cumulative and intersecting effects of neurodivergence, chronic illness,  ethnicity, gender)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 745 0 R
/Next 766 0 R
>>
endobj
766 0 obj
<<
/Title (lived trauma, and environmental compatibility. These limit--ations are particularly evident in the experiences of women from ethnically diverse backgrounds, whose narratives frequently fall outside standardised diagnostic frameworks. Lived-experience research has demonstrated that misinter- -pretation, over-pathologisation & insufficient cultural res-  -ponsiveness may exacerbate distress rather than facilitate recovery. This study examines mental health diversity within the context of an interracial family of four \(three males and one female, aged 45, 35, 14, and 10\) of British, Polish, Caribbean, and African heritage.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 745 0 R
/Prev 765 0 R
>>
endobj
