818 0 obj
<<
/Title (Mental health discourse has historically struggled to distin--guish normative emotional dysregulation from clinically significant psychiatric pathology. This conceptual ambiguity has, at times, contributed to the over pathologisation of adaptive responses to trauma, chronic stress exposure, and unmet developmental needs \(Herman, 1992; van der Kolk, 2014\). Contemporary trauma and developmental research increasingly emphasises that many behavioural & affective disturbances reflect nervous system adaptations to envi-       -ronmental threat rather than primary disease processes \(Porges, 2011; Schore, 2003\). While the doctoral research of Duchess Dr. Letitia Kapuscinska provides an empirical framework for trauma-informed, voluntary, and integrative mental health care, the present contribution offers an experiential and pedagogical translation model designed to support emotional insight, self regulation, and participatory recovery. The Potted Plant Method is introduced as a trauma informed, neuro emotional mapping framework that)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 796 0 R
>>
endobj
819 0 obj
<<
/Title (Dr. Letitia Antoinette)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 772 0 R
/Prev 796 0 R
/Next 797 0 R
>>
endobj
820 0 obj
<<
/Title (renders complex nervous-system and attachment processes accessible through visual and developmental metaphor. Rather than construing cyclical emotional states as inheren-  -tly pathological, the framework conceptualises fluctuations such as depression, frustration, inspiration, & resilience as adaptive autonomic responses to relational & environmental conditions. This position is consistent with contemporary models of autonomic regulation and stress responsivity \(Porges, 2011; Siegel, 2012\))
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 797 0 R
>>
endobj
